.TH TMUX-COPY 1 "2026-08-29" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmux-copy \- copy stdin to the best available clipboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B command
.B |
.B tmux-copy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads all of stdin and hands it to the first working clipboard mechanism.
The detection order is:
.TP
.B pbcopy
macOS.
.TP
.B clip.exe
WSL2. Run by its absolute path
.RI ( /mnt/c/Windows/System32/clip.exe )
and only when
.I /proc/version
says this really is a Microsoft-hosted kernel \(em a cross-mounted
.I /mnt/c
on a bare-metal Linux box does not count.
.TP
.B wl-copy
Wayland sessions.
.TP
.B xclip
X11 sessions, writing to the CLIPBOARD selection.
.PP
Detection is by capability, not by platform: each candidate is checked with
.BR command \-v (or
its absolute path for
.BR clip.exe )
so a single unmodified copy of the script works on macOS, Linux, WSL2 and
SSH hosts alike. A candidate that fails (exit status non-zero) is skipped and
the next one is tried.
.TP
.B "No clipboard at all: OSC 52"
When no clipboard binary is present, the data is base64-encoded (busybox
compatible
.BR base64 )
and emitted as an OSC 52 escape sequence with the
.B c
clipboard selector and a BEL terminator. Capable terminals receive the copy
even over ssh; in tmux this requires
.B set-clipboard external
and
.BR "allow-passthrough on" ,
which
.I .tmux.conf
sets.
.PP
The script does not capture the clipboard helpers' output; they are standard
clipboard tools that are silent on success, and success is judged by their
exit status.
.PP
Empty input skips every candidate (all of them are guarded by a non-empty
check) and falls through to the OSC 52 emission, which then transmits an
empty payload.
.SH USAGE
.B .tmux.conf
binds the copy-mode yank and Enter keys to
.B copy-pipe-and-cancel
with
.B tmux-copy
as the pipe command, so every yank lands in the native clipboard of the
terminal client machine, whatever it runs.
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy the current tmux selection (the normal path, nothing to type), or
manually from a shell:
.PP
.nf
    $ printf 'hello clipboard' | tmux-copy
    $ tail -n 100 /var/log/system.log | tmux-copy
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 in practice: every code path ends successfully because OSC 52 emission,
the final fallback, is a plain
.B printf
that always succeeds, even with no clipboard tool present. A failing
clipboard candidate never aborts the script; its non-zero status merely
triggers the next candidate in line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR loadavg (1),
.BR dots\-setup (1)